 "standard_text": "Work with sequences including those given by a formula for the nth term and by a recurrence relation; understand and use sigma notation; arithmetic and geometric sequences and series, including the sum to infinity of a convergent geometric series.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 13 A level maths worksheets on sequences and series: arithmetic and geometric series, sum to infinity, sigma notation and the nth term. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Arithmetic sum: Sₙ = n/2 (2a + (n − 1)d).",
 "key_sub": "Geometric sum to infinity: a/(1 − r), when |r| < 1.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>Arithmetic: <i>u<sub>n</sub></i> = <i>a</i> + (<i>n</i> − 1)<i>d</i>, S<sub>n</sub> = ½<i>n</i>(2<i>a</i> + (<i>n</i> − 1)<i>d</i>). Geometric: <i>u<sub>n</sub></i> = <i>ar</i><sup>n−1</sup>, S<sub>n</sub> = <i>a</i>(1 − <i>r</i><sup>n</sup>)/(1 − <i>r</i>), S<sub>∞</sub> = <i>a</i>/(1 − <i>r</i>) for |<i>r</i>| &lt; 1.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Sum to infinity of 8 + 4 + 2 + …</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>a</i> = 8, <i>r</i> = ½</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>8 ÷ ½</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 16</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Using n instead of n − 1 in the nth term.</li><li>Using the sum to infinity when |r| ≥ 1.</li><li>Counting the terms in a sigma sum wrongly.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "When does a geometric series have a sum to infinity?",
   "a": "Only when the common ratio is between −1 and 1. Then S∞ = a/(1 − r)."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does Σ mean?",
   "a": "Sigma means add up: Σ from k = 1 to 5 of k² = 1 + 4 + 9 + 16 + 25."
  }
 ],
